description:
card.rs:395-404:

    let source_id = match spec_val.get("id").and_then(|v| v.as_str()) {
        Some(s) => Id::from(s),
        None => {
            not_copied.insert(create_id, json!({ "type": "invalidProperties", "description": "id is required in copy spec" }));
            continue;
        }
    };

clippy::pedantic flags this as single_match_else. The idiomatic Rust form is:

    let Some(s) = spec_val.get("id").and_then(|v| v.as_str()) else {
        not_copied.insert(create_id, json!({ "type": "invalidProperties", "description": "id is required in copy spec" }));
        continue;
    };
    let source_id = Id::from(s);

The let-else (stable since Rust 1.65) is exactly the construct for "extract a value, otherwise diverge". The current match-with-continue-in-None-arm pattern is the pre-1.65 workaround.

Same pattern recurs at card.rs:229-232:

    let id_str = match id_val.as_str() {
        Some(s) => s.to_owned(),
        None => continue, // unreachable: validated above
    };

This second one is even worse: the comment says "unreachable: validated above". The right idiom is .expect("validated above as string") so the unreachable case at least panics audibly rather than skipping silently.

Engineering cost: trivial. Filed as clippy-grade. The match-vs-if-let-else question is settled idiom since Rust 1.65; using the older form in greenfield code is a tells-you-the-author-has-not-updated-their-mental-model signal more than a real cost.
